  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Women in Defence of Social Rights: Jubileu Sul</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project will build the capacity of poor and excluded women and men living in Fortaleza and S&#xe3;o Paulo to understand, claim and defend their social rights, particularly to health and social protection, in a context in which these constitutionally guaranteed rights are threatened by extreme austerity measures, dismantling of key institutions, regressive policy changes and growing social intolerance and discrimination of the poorest. Direct beneficiaries will include former prisoners, indigenous people, young people and informal waste management workers, of whom at least 60% will be women. This will be achieved by training and accompanying autonomous groups of women and indigenous people using &apos;social mapping&apos; and participatory research to enable beneficiaries to identify the roots of their exclusion, understand their rights and the changes proposed to the constitutional rights, and to develop their capacity/skills to promote change in their reality, strengthening their organisations and preparing them to participate and advocate in local and national spaces for social accountability. The training will be supplemented by development of didactic materials. They will link with local and national church networks and CSOs with common interests and together they will hold local and national authorities to account for decisions that affect the social rights of the most vulnerable. Members of their communities will benefit indirectly by information sharing  and ways of accessing to social rights, ensuring the poorest have access to social benefits - such as maternity benefit, and defending existing services - such as health facilities, and reducing or barring the impact of policy changes which will most negatively impact the poorest.</narrative>
  </description>
